body:
A similar but slightly different use case which is also effected by this issue. In this use case, I'm trying to add authentication to groups of routes.

```go
package main

import (
	"log"

	"github.com/gofiber/fiber/v2"
)

func findUser() map[string]interface{} {
	// actually lookup the user
	return nil
}

func requireAuth(c *fiber.Ctx) error {
	u := findUser()

	if u == nil {
		return c.SendStatus(fiber.StatusForbidden)
	}

	c.Locals("user", u)
	return c.Next()
}

func main() {
	app := fiber.New()

	lists := fiber.New()
	lists.Get("/", func(c *fiber.Ctx) error {
		return c.SendStatus(fiber.StatusOK)
	})

	users := fiber.New()
	users.Get("/", func(c *fiber.Ctx) error {
		return c.SendStatus(fiber.StatusOK)
	})

	api := app.Group("/api")

	api.Get("/foo", func(c *fiber.Ctx) error {
		return c.SendStatus(fiber.StatusOK)
	})

	api.Mount("/lists", lists)

	authorized := api.Group("/", requireAuth)

	authorized.Get("/bar", func(c *fiber.Ctx) error {
		return c.SendStatus(fiber.StatusOK)
	})

	authorized.Mount("/users", users)

	api.Get("/baz", func(c *fiber.Ctx) error {
		return c.SendStatus(fiber.StatusOK)
	})

	log.Fatal(app.Listen(":3000"))
}
```

I found that this used to work up until v2.40.0 was released. Here is a list of the endpoints and responses:

```
# what I would have expected to happen from reading the code
GET /api/foo -> 200
GET /api/lists -> 200
GET /api/bar -> 403
GET /api/users -> 403
GET /api/baz -> 200

# actual behavior v2.39.0 and below
GET /api/foo -> 200
GET /api/lists -> 200
GET /api/bar -> 403
GET /api/users -> 403
GET /api/baz -> 403

# actual behavior v2.40.0 and above
GET /api/foo -> 200
GET /api/lists -> 403
GET /api/bar -> 403
GET /api/users -> 403
GET /api/baz -> 403
```

Is there a different way this is meant to be done? Or an actual bug with the way I'm trying to use `.Group()` and `.Mount()`?

My current workaround to this was to add a `.Use()` statement to the users app, like:

```go
users := fiber.New()
users.Use(requireAuth)
users.Get("/", func(c *fiber.Ctx) error {
	return c.SendStatus(fiber.StatusOK)
})
```

The downside to this approach, is that because I organize groups of routes into their own packages, I then have to add the same middleware(s) to each one (across many files) instead of being able to more easily apply them once at the top.
